## Essential behavior

- Unix stream JSON lines; preserve response order and correlation per client.
  Terminal events are best effort, after durable receipt and admission response.
  Disconnect never cancels. Shutdown drains accepted work and exits zero.
- Limits: 8 clients, 40 MiB request text, 64 items/16 MiB per adapter batch,
  4 MiB and 4,096 combined items per transaction; admission 1,024 outstanding
  jobs AND 64 MiB raw payload. Running and completed-but-uncommitted work counts.
- Validate framing/fields/IDs/lowercase even hex before changing state. Delegate
  Bitcoin parsing/encoding entirely to the adapter. Integer wire fields are
  decimal strings; digests are hex in the specified byte order.
- Retry identity is SHA-256 of ordered mode byte, operation byte, u64 LE input
  length, raw bytes, repeated for every item. Same ID/digest returns existing;
  different digest conflicts. Keep the ID index forever.
- Admission persists complete payload, sequence, digest and accounting atomically
  before accepted. Cancellation persists intent before ok. Terminal transitions
  commit strictly by admission sequence, atomically writing receipt, deleting
  pending/cancel keys, advancing checkpoint and releasing accounting.
- Cancellation boundary is entry into the interposed C terminal write call,
  not completion of computation, socket arrival, or the later write barrier.
  A completed cancellation before that boundary must win.
- Schema 1: meta/version, sequence, checkpoint, jobs, bytes; id/ID; p/SEQ,
  c/SEQ, r/SEQ with exactly 20-digit SEQ. Initialize version durably; missing
  counters mean zero only for a fresh DB. Recover pending payloads/cancellations;
  reject incompatible schema. Every sequence through checkpoint has a receipt;
  every subsequent admitted sequence has a pending record.
- All transitions use dynamic RocksDB C WriteBatch, WAL enabled and sync=true.
  Required settings: no compression, 128 MiB cache, 64 MiB write buffer, two
  buffers/two background jobs. Native open supplies exclusive writer locking.
  Storage failure stops with diagnostics, never acknowledges uncommitted state.

## Ownership and implementation direction (unimplemented)

- Prefer one state/storage owner and four workers; create DB on its owner thread
  to avoid unsupported Send/Sync assertions on reuse raw pointers. Serialize
  cancellation/terminal decisions with writes. Keep slow socket output separate.
- Use repr(C) adapter structures. V1 item pointers must point into arena data;
  keep input immutable and arena alive until all native calls return, including
  cancellation. Caller owns results. Null arena: evaluate execution_failure;
  acknowledged job: stop and retain pending state for restart.
- Docs confirm UnixStream clones share socket/options, sync_channel can block,
  try_send does not block, and read_line/read_until need explicit size bounds.
  Vec::try_reserve_exact and thread::Builder::spawn expose allocation/spawn
  failures. CString pointer lifetime must outlast native calls.
- Reuse provides open/get/write/batches, not iteration, recovery, scheduling or
  service policy. Add any needed C iterator declarations from the allowed header.
- Tests next phase should target recovery and DB invariants, cancellation races,
  four-slot concurrency with ordered commits, retries/admission limits, slow
  clients, exclusive locking, and draining. Public smoke test is insufficient.

